You can use the following RegEx expression to match the "@@@" comments and then convert the comment into a Debug.Assert statement, replacing "\n" with "@n":

```cs
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

...

// Matching "@@@" comments
string regexPattern = @"/\*\*\s@\@\@\s(.*?)\*/"; 
// Matching anything, until new line symbol "\n" or end of line 
string patternReplace = @"Debug.Assert($1);"; 

Regex r = new Regex(regexPattern);
Match m = r.Match(inputString);

while (m.Success)
{
    string comment = m.Groups[1].Value;
    // Replacing new line symbols "\n" with "@n"
    comment = comment.Replace("\n", "@n"); 
    string replaced = m.Result(comment);
    
    Console.WriteLine(replaced);

    m = m.NextMatch();
}
```

This script will process every matching "@@@" comment in the csharp code and convert it into a Debug.Assert statement. Be aware that it will process every comment (not every word starting with @@@). It also assumes that @ signs inside comments are @@. When used in a real C# code it should be adjusted according to actual programming conventions. It requires C# 7.0 and above because of '$1'.

Also notice that the "PexObserve.ValueForViewing" statements don't require to be converted into Debug.Assert like the others, they are used only during PEX testing.

