Debug.--------------------In C#, we use the Debug.Assert method for assertions/checking. However, the code sample that you provided uses the output-checking annotations @@@, which we won't be able to capture using Debug.Assert statements. You can't directly "output" assert statements, as these statements are primarily used for detecting logic errors.

To get the assertions inside your C# code, you can take advantage of the asserts provided by your testing/development environment, like NUnit, which has the Assert class in the NUnit.Framework namespace. Here's an example:

```CSharp
using NUnit.Framework;

[Test]
public void MyTestMethod()
{
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();
    int x = 5;
    arrList.Add(x);
    
    // These are assertions, check if the added value is there
    Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
    Assert.AreEqual(arrList.IndexOf(x), 0);
    Assert.AreEqual(arrList.Count, 1);
}
```

You can adjust the conditions according to your requirements and assert the expected changes in your array list.

If you still prefer doing this via csharp/runtime, you might have use .Net framework libraries or some kind of assertion library. 

Here is an example of checking for null values and negative values in csharp:

```CSharp
public void MyMethod() 
{
  Debug.Assert(value != null, "The value cannot be null");
  Debug.Assert(value >= 0, "The value must be a positive number");
}
```--------------------Please see the below assertion code block. It corresponds to the comments starting with "@@@".

```csharp
[Test]
public void VerifyIfReturnOnFailureOfContainsContractIsMinusOne()
{
    List<int> list = new List<int>();
    
    // Execute code here
    int New_Ret = list.IndexOf(-1);
    
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1);
}
```

In the place where it says, "// Execute code here", you would put your code execution for PUT_ContainsContract method. 

I've created a simple Assert-condition based on the comment for PUT_ContainsContract method. Please adapt it to your code.

Remember to add the NUnit library to your project if you aren't already doing so:

```xml
<PackageReference Include="NUnit" Version="3.13.1" />
```--------------------```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    //... rest of the method
        
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    Debug.Assert(!New_arrListContainsX == !Old_arrListContainsX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    //... rest of the method

    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - 1);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);
    Debug.Assert(!New_arrListContainsX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0);

    //... rest of the method

    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_arrListIndexOfX == index);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    Debug.Assert(!New_arrListContainsX == !Old_arrListContainsX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

    //... rest of the method
    
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_arrListIndexOfX == index);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == index);
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

    //... rest of the method

    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
    Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    //... rest of the method

    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListContainsX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    //... rest of the method

    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    //... rest of the method

    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    //... rest of the assertions
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount); // @@@ Number of elements should not change if operation is successful or unsuccessful
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX); // @@@ The index of x in old list should equal the index of x in new list
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX); // @@@ The last index of x in old list should equal the last index of x in new list
Debug.Assert((New_Ret == Old_arrListCount || New_Ret == Old_arrListCount + 1) && Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX); // @@@ Return value should either be the count of elements or count + 1, and element must be present if return value != count. 
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == New_Ret); // @@@ new indexOf x must equal the return value
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_Ret); // @@@ new lastIndexOf x greater or equal to the return value
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount > 0 || New_Ret == 0); // @@@ count after an operation in non-empty list should be greater than or equal to 1
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 || New_Ret < Old_arrListCount); // @@@ If operation is not successful last position of x in new list is exactly -1 or less than previous count of the list
```

Please note that the comments starting with '@@@' correspond directly to the `Assert` statements specified in the code snippet. This code assumes that 'Old_' and 'New_' prefixes represent the states of the `ArrayList` objects before and after specific operations. Replace the prefixes or variable names in the method bodies with your program variables if they have different names.--------------------```csharp
// @@@ If ret value of arrList.Add() is equal to Old_arrListCount, new list count increases by one 
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);

// @@@ If the new list count is decreased by 1, the old list count must also decrease by one
Debug.Assert((New_Ret == 0 && New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount) || New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount -1);

// @@@ If index is greater than or equal to zero and less than arrList.Count, last index of x is equal to arrList.Count -1 
Debug.Assert(Old_index >= 0 && Old_index < Old_arrListCount);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount -1);

// @@@ If index is less than zero, last index of x in new list is less than old list count
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX < Math.Min(Old_index, Old_arrListCount) || New_arrListLastIndexOfX < 0);

// @@@ If the return value is true, the x is present in the list 
Debug.Assert(!Old_arrListContainsX || New_Ret);

// @@@ If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1 
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListLastIndexOfX > Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ If x is present in the arrList.IndexOf, the return value is not less than zero
Debug.Assert((Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 || New_Ret >= 0));

// @@@ If the return value of arrList.IndexOf(x) is greater than arrList.Count or if the x is not present in the list, the return value of arrList.LastIndexOf(x) is also equal to the size of the arrList. 
Debug.Assert((New_arrListIndexOfX < 0 || (New_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListCount)) || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListCount);

// @@@ If size of arrList before equals size of arrList after, the return value of arrList.IndexOf(x) also equals to the return value of arrList.LastIndexOf(x) 
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount || New_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ If the x is present in the arrList, the return value of arrList.Contains(x) is true 
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 || New_Ret);
```

The code mentioned the assertion statements which are present in the `PUT_AddContract`, `PUT_RemoveContract`, `PUT_InsertContract`, `PUT_SetContract`, `PUT_GetContract`, `PUT_ContainsContract`, `PUT_IndexOfContract`, `PUT_LastIndexOfContract`, `PUT_CountContract` methods respectively. In each method, different assertions are made which test the preconditions and postconditions of the ArrayList class methods.--------------------C# contains the Debug.Assert method that can be used for verifying conditions. This method throws an AssertFailedException when the condition is false.

Here's a conversion of your comments into these C# assert statements:

```cs
   [PexMethod]
   public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
   {
       // @@@ If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1 
       int currentLastIndex = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
       Assert.IsTrue(currentLastIndex == -1);

       // rest of the code...
   }
```

Please note that Pex or Pex/Rational Purify software should be used in unit testing/verification before you can use Assert statements. This would prevent you from performing unecessary assertions and hence improve performance.--------------------Here are the output assert statements corresponding to the comments in the code: 

If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1
```csharp
[Test]
public void TestLastIndexOf()
{
    var arrList = new ArrayList();
    int x = 5;

    arrList.Add(x);
    int newRet = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    
    Assert.AreEqual(-1, newRet); // @@@ If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1 
}
```

When an item is removed the LastIndexOf of that item should still be as per previous one, not -1
```csharp
[Test]
public void TestLastIndexOfAfterRemove()
{
    var arrList = new ArrayList();
    int x = 5;
    int y = 6;
    
    arrList.Add(x);
    arrList.Add(y);

    arrList.Remove(x);

    int oldRet = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    int newRet = arrList.LastIndexOf(y);
    
    Assert.AreEqual(-1, oldRet); // When an item is removed the LastIndexOf of that item should still be as per previous one, not -1
    Assert.IsTrue(newRet >= 0); // After remove, y should have some index >= 0
}
```

If the IndexOf the removed element gives -1, the Count should reduce by 1
```csharp
[Test]
public void TestCountAfterRemove()
{
    var arrList = new ArrayList();
    int x = 5;
    
    arrList.Add(x);
    arrList.Remove(x);
    
    int oldIndex = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
   
    Assert.AreEqual(-1, oldIndex); // If the IndexOf the removed element gives -1, the Count should reduce by 1
    Assert.AreEqual(0, oldCount);
}
```

Index should not be greater than the Count
```csharp
[Test]
public void TestInsertContract()
{
    var arrList = new ArrayList();
    int x = 5;
    int index = 5;

    arrList.Add(x);
    arrList.Insert(5, 7);

    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
    int newIndex = 5;
   
    Assert.IsTrue(newIndex <= oldCount); // Index should not be greater than the Count
}

```

LastIndexOf and IndexOf should behave as expected for items newly added
```csharp
[Test]
public void TestNewAddItems()
{
    var arrList = new ArrayList();
    int x = 5;
    int y = 6;

    // Add x and y
    arrList.Add(x);
    arrList.Add(y);

    int oldXIndex = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int oldYIndex = arrList.IndexOf(y);
    
    int newListCount = arrList.Count;

    Assert.IsTrue(oldXIndex < newListCount /2); // IndexOf(x) should be less than Count/2
    Assert.IsTrue(oldYIndex > newListCount /2); // IndexOf(y) should be greater than Count/2

    int oldXLastPos = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    int oldYLastPos = arrList.LastIndexOf(y);
    
    Assert.IsTrue(oldXLastPos ==  oldXIndex); // LastIndexOf(x) should be IndexOf(x) for a new addition
    Assert.IsTrue(oldYLastPos == (newListCount -1)); // LastIndexOf(y) should be Count -1 for a new addition
}
```

